#
# TLS for the node, and for every handle it issues.
#
# ⭐ **On-demand TLS is not an optimization here, it is what makes a handle namespace
# work.** A handle on this network is a domain name, so a server issuing handles has to
# answer on a hostname per account — and it cannot know them in advance, because they are
# created by people signing up. Enumerating them into a certificate list would mean
# reissuing on every signup. Instead Caddy asks the server whether a name it has just been
# asked for is one of ours, and gets a certificate on the spot if it is.
#
# 🚨 **`ask` is a gate rather than a formality, and removing it turns this into an open
# certificate mill.** Without it, anybody who points a DNS record at this machine makes it
# request a certificate on their behalf, which is rate-limited by the certificate authority
# and will eventually take the real names down with it.
{
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://localhost:3000/tls-check
	}
}

# The apex answers the protocol; everything under it is somebody's handle.
*.{$PDS_HOSTNAME}, {$PDS_HOSTNAME} {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy http://localhost:3000
}

# The public status page — served from this droplet on purpose, a failure domain apart
# from the hub it reports on. The heartbeat's cron regenerates /pds/status/index.html
# every minute (scripts/status-page.sh), so this site serves a static file and answers
# whether or not the hub does: the reader checking whether anthers.org is down gets an
# answer even during the outage the question is about.
#
# ⚠️ **The name is not a handle and must not look like one.** It lives outside the
# wildcard's namespace (`status.anthers.org` does not end in `.anthers.social`), so the
# on-demand gate below cannot be tricked into minting it — it is named here explicitly,
# which is the Caddy form of "we meant this one".
#
# 🚨 **The DNS record for this name must exist and point here** (see the Runbook's § on
# this page): a Caddyfile site for a name no DNS record carries mints nothing.
#
# ⚠️ **No `tls` directive at all, rather than an empty `tls {}` block** — the empty block
# is invalid Caddyfile syntax ("wrong argument count or unexpected line ending after '}'",
# learned by crash-looping the live Caddy for twenty seconds on 2026-10-08), and it is not
# needed: a site address spelling `https://` gets automatic HTTPS with a normally-issued
# certificate, and this name is named explicitly here (not under the wildcard's
# `on_demand`), so it provisions at startup like any ordinary first-site cert.
https://status.anthers.org {
	encode zstd gzip
	root * /pds/status
	file_server
	header / {
		# The page answers per minute, never worth caching at an intermediary.
		Cache-Control "no-cache"
		-Server
	}
}
